  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Price Survey Sheet",
    "overview": "Rego Pasar is drawn as an Indonesian topographic map sheet (Peta Rupabumi). The DIY average price is sea level; each regency is land or sea by how far its price sits above or below it. The national average stays as a number in the comparison table. A deep sea-blue collar band carries the title and region tabs, a near-white sheet holds square, hairline-ruled panels, and all colour that is not ink belongs either to the hypsometric scale or to the rise/fall pair.\n\nThe page is dense and calm, the density of a printed survey sheet rather than a dashboard. Depth comes from rules and tints, not from cards floating on shadows. Lettering follows map practice: condensed, letterspaced caps for place names and panel titles, a heavy upright grotesque for the one answer headline, and tabular numerals everywhere a price appears.\n\nThe signature is that the map and the chart are instruments, not decoration: the map is the region selector and the trend chart is an elevation profile with contour lines.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One typeface (Archivo Variable), varied by width and weight, never by family.",
      "Square corners on every surface and control.",
      "Hairline rules (1px) separate; a 2px ink rule opens a block.",
      "Seven-step hypsometric ramp is the only categorical colour scale.",
      "Direction always carries an SVG arrow and a colour together.",
      "Light and dark themes follow the device; both are defined token-for-token."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Sea Level Rule",
        "body": "Blue means cheaper than the DIY average; sand, ochre and brown mean dearer. The ramp is used only for regency price against the DIY average. Difference from national is a level, not a movement: it is set in Ink with an explicit sign and a percentage, never in Rise Red or Fall Blue.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Arrow-and-Colour Rule",
        "body": "Rise Red and Fall Blue mark change over time only, and never appear on a number without the matching SVG arrow. Colour is never the only signal.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` is set on body; every price column aligns digit for digit.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Family Rule",
        "body": "Contrast comes from width (wdth 62–125) and weight (100–900) of Archivo. No second family.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Paper Rule",
        "body": "Panels sit on the sheet, they do not float. Use a 1px Rule border, not a shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** colour regency price against the DIY average only with the seven-step hypsometric ramp, banded at ±3, ±10, ±20 percent.",
      "**Do** pair every rise or fall with an SVG arrow and Rise Red / Fall Blue.",
      "**Do** use condensed letterspaced caps (wdth 70–80, 0.07–0.1em) for map names, panel titles, column heads, and tabs.",
      "**Do** keep every corner square and separate with 1px Rule hairlines; open a block with a 2px Ink rule.",
      "**Do** define every new colour in both the light and dark token sets.",
      "**Do** show \"tidak ada data\" in italic Ink Muted wherever a price is null."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** add rounded corners, card shadows, or gradient fills to panels; the readout is the only shadow.",
      "**Don't** reuse the ramp tints for categories, status, or decoration.",
      "**Don't** introduce a second typeface; vary Archivo's width and weight instead.",
      "**Don't** show direction by colour alone.",
      "**Don't** build equal KPI tiles; the answer headline leads, the map compares."
    ]
  }
